'''book''' (bu̇k), ''v.'' [< ME. ''boken'', < ''book'', n.; cf. AS. ''bōcian'', give by charter (= OFries. ''bōkia'' = Icel. ''bōka''), < ''bōc'', book, charter: see ''book'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To convey by book or charter.
{{smaller block|It was an infringement of the law to ''book'' family or hereditary lands.
{{right|''H. Cabot Lodge.''}}}}
'''2.''' To enter, write, or register in a book; record.
{{smaller block|Let it be ''booked'' with the rest of this day’s deeds.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iv. 3.}}
I always from my youth have endeavoured to get the rarest secrets, and ''book'' them. ''B. Jonson'', Volpone, ii. 1.}}
'''3.''' To enter in a list; enroll; enlist for service.
{{smaller block|This indeede (Eudoxus) hath bene hitherto, and yet is, a common order amongest them, to have all the people ''booked'' by the lordes and gentellmen, but yet it is the woorst order that ever was devised.
{{right|''Spenser'', State of Ireland.}}}}
'''4.''' To engage or secure beforehand by registry or payment, as a seat in a stage-coach or a box at the opera.—'''5.''' To deliver, and pay for the transmission of, as a parcel or merchandise: as, the luggage was ''booked'' through to London.—'''6.''' To reserve accommodation for; receive, and undertake to forward: as, at that office passengers (or parcels) were ''booked'' to all parts of the world. [In senses 4, 5, and 6, confined to the British islands.]—'''7.''' To make into a book, as gold-leaf, tobacco-leaves, etc. {{smaller|—'''Booked at last''', caught and disposed of.}}

